.TH SYSTEM-UPDATE 1 "September 2026" "system-update 1.0.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
system-update \- keep Ubuntu, snaps, Flatpak apps and firmware up to date
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B system-update
.br
.B system-update check
.RB [ \-\-refresh ]
.RB [ \-\-no\-firmware ]
.RB [ \-\-json ]
.br
.B system-update upgrade
.RB [ \-y ]
.RB [ \-n ]
.RB [ \-v ]
.RI [ options ]
.br
.B system-update history
.RB [ \-\-json ]
.br
.B system-update
.RB [ \-\-yes ]
.RB [ \-\-dry\-run ]
.RB [ \-\-no\-firmware ]
.RB [ \-\-verbose ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B system-update
installs updates for system packages (APT), snaps, Flatpak apps and device
firmware (fwupd), then tidies up: it removes unused packages, the package
cache, old snap revisions and unused Flatpak runtimes, and trims the system
journal.
.PP
Run without arguments to open the desktop app.
Run as root without arguments, it updates the system like
.BR "system-update upgrade" .
The last form above is the command line of the older
.B system-update.sh
script, which keeps working, so
.B system-update
can replace it in timers and cron jobs.
.PP
Checking never changes anything and needs no password.
Updating is done by a helper that runs as root through
.BR pkexec (1),
so you are asked for an administrator password once per run, unless you
are already root.
Nothing ever stops to ask a question, and apt and dpkg are never
interrupted, not even by a timeout.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B check
List the available updates for every source.
Nothing is changed.
With
.BR \-\-refresh ,
package information is refreshed first, which the desktop app also does when
it opens; on an installed system that needs no password.
.TP
.B upgrade
Refresh package information, install all updates and clean up.
In order, it runs:
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
.BR "APT" :
wait for other package managers, refresh package lists, finish interrupted
installations
.RB ( "dpkg \-\-configure \-a" ", " "apt-get \-f install" ),
then
.BR "apt-get full-upgrade" ,
keeping existing configuration files.
.IP \(bu 2
.BR "Snaps" :
refresh every snap through snapd.
.IP \(bu 2
.BR "Flatpak" :
update the system installation, and yours when you are not root.
.IP \(bu 2
.BR "Firmware" :
refresh fwupd metadata and list available firmware updates.
They are only installed with
.BR \-\-yes .
.IP \(bu 2
.BR "Clean-up" :
.BR "apt-get autoremove \-\-purge" ,
.BR "apt-get clean" ,
removal of disabled snap revisions,
.BR "flatpak uninstall \-\-unused" ,
and
.B journalctl \-\-rotate \-\-vacuum\-time \-\-vacuum\-size
(7 days and 200 MB by default).
.RE
.TP
.B history
List past update runs, including the ones started by timers or as root.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-y ", " \-\-yes
Fully unattended: also install firmware updates, and let
.BR needrestart (1)
restart services that use outdated libraries instead of only listing them.
.TP
.BR \-n ", " \-\-dry\-run
Show what would be updated and which steps would run, and stop.
Needs no password.
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-verbose
Print the output of every command as it runs.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-firmware
Skip firmware checks and updates entirely.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-snap
Do not update snaps or remove old snap revisions.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-flatpak
Do not update Flatpak apps or remove unused runtimes.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-cleanup
Skip every clean-up step.
.TP
.BI \-\-journal\-days " DAYS"
Keep this many days of system journal (1\(en365).
.TP
.BI \-\-journal\-max\-mb " MB"
Trim the system journal to at most this many megabytes (16\(en100000).
.TP
.B \-\-refresh
Refresh package information before checking
.RB ( check
only).
.TP
.B \-\-json
Print machine-readable JSON.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-color
Never use colors. Colors are also off when output is not a terminal or
.B NO_COLOR
is set.
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-version
Print the version and exit.
.PP
Without options, the journal limits saved in the desktop app's preferences
are used.
.SH SAFETY
Only one update runs at a time; it shares the lock
.I /run/lock/system-update.lock
with
.BR system-update.sh ,
so the two never run together.
Before touching packages it waits up to 5 minutes for other package managers,
such as unattended-upgrades, to finish.
The privileged helper accepts only a fixed list of task names and numeric
limits, and never runs commands or paths supplied by the caller.
Pressing Ctrl+C, or Stop in the app, finishes the current step and skips
the rest.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /var/log/system-update/update-YYYYMMDD-HHMMSS.log
A detailed log of each run, in the same format as
.BR system-update.sh .
The 10 most recent logs are kept.
They can be read by root and members of the
.B adm
group.
.TP
.I /var/lib/system-update/history.jsonl
The record of past runs shown by
.B history
and in the app.
.TP
.I ~/.config/system-update/settings.json
Preferences and the selection made in the app.
.TP
.I /usr/libexec/system-update/system-update-helper
The privileged helper.
.TP
.I /usr/share/polkit-1/actions/io.github.wakedog.SystemUpdate.policy
Polkit policy: checking for updates needs no password in an active local
session; installing asks for an administrator password.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
For
.BR check :
everything is up to date.
.TP
.B 1
Some steps failed (see the log), or
.B check
could not check a source.
.TP
.B 2
Invalid arguments.
.TP
.B 3
The update could not start: another update is running, there is no network
connection, or authentication failed.
.TP
.B 100
.B check
found updates.
.TP
.B 130
Interrupted.
.SH EXAMPLES
See what is available:
.PP
.RS
.B system-update check
.RE
.PP
Update everything from a nightly timer, including firmware:
.PP
.RS
.B system-update \-\-yes
.RE
.PP
Warn from a monitoring script when security updates are waiting:
.PP
.RS
.B system-update check \-\-json | jq .security
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR apt-get (8),
.BR snap (8),
.BR flatpak (1),
.BR fwupdmgr (1),
.BR journalctl (1),
.BR pkexec (1)
